\section{Introduction}
Training recurrent neural networks to handle long-term dependencies remains a significant challenge. This difficulty is evident in the vanishing and exploding gradient problems, which hinder the learning of long-term dependencies in recurrent neural networks (RNNs). To mitigate these issues, various methods have been proposed, including hierarchical RNNs, LSTM, and GRU. Despite these advancements, training RNNs on long-term dependency tasks continues to be challenging.
In this work, we introduce the "attentional cell," a new component designed to be inserted into an LSTM network. This cell combines the architecture of LSTM with a self-attention mechanism, enabling the network to concentrate on specific time steps within the sequence. Consequently, this design allows the network to effectively learn long-term dependencies.
We demonstrate that networks with attentional cells perform better than those with standard LSTMs. Furthermore, we show that inserting attentional cells into an LSTM network can further improve performance.
